  (:adr/body "2026-09-01. A faucet for itonami, gated by a passkey rather than a CAPTCHA, and
issuing a grant rather than only sending tokens.

## Why this came up

The testnet buyer is built and cannot be funded. Every Base Sepolia faucet
gates on a login or a CAPTCHA, and an agent must do neither -- one is
credential entry, the other is bot-detection bypass. So the last step of an
autonomous purchase is blocked by a human-verification puzzle placed in front
of a machine.

That is not an obstacle to route around. It is a signal that the CAPTCHA is
measuring the wrong thing.

## A CAPTCHA gates the wrong party

A CAPTCHA asks: is a human here. For a faucet whose consumer is an agent, the
only two outcomes are that a human solved it on the agent's behalf, in which
case the puzzle measured nothing about the agent, or that the agent bypassed
it, in which case it measured nothing at all. Either way the answer it returns
is unrelated to the question a faucet actually has, which is whether this draw
should count against somebody.

## A passkey answers a question a faucet can use

auth.itonami.cloud already runs passkey sign-in and publishes a full OAuth
discovery document: authorize, token, introspect, userinfo. The faucet can be a
relying party of it and implement no ceremony crypto of its own.

What a passkey assertion establishes is narrower than identity and more useful
than a CAPTCHA: a specific authenticator was present and its holder made a
gesture. Draws are counted per credential, not per address and not per IP. An
address is free to make and an IP is cheap to rotate; a credential costs a
device.

**The honest claim, stated so nobody overreads it**: a passkey proves one
authenticator, not one person. Someone with several devices gets several
allowances, and the faucet should say that rather than imply an identity it
cannot see. What it buys is a rate limit that survives address rotation, which
is the property a faucet needs.

## The faucet issues the grant, which is why it belongs here

A faucet that sends tokens to an address is a utility. This one issues the
spend grant from ADR-2609010100 in the same act:

  a person draws, once, with a passkey
  the faucet funds the session address AND records the grant it may spend under
  the agent spends within that grant, and never touches the passkey

That resolves the contradiction that a passkey cannot sign a payment. It does
not have to. It signs the grant, rarely, with a person present, which is
exactly the root-and-holder shape the biscuit work already has. The faucet is
the first issuer of those grants, and issuing them is what makes it load
bearing rather than a convenience.

## What it must refuse

An assertion it cannot verify is refused, not treated as anonymous -- a forged
token and no token must not produce the same response. A credential over its
allowance is refused with the time it may return, because a refusal that does
not say when is indistinguishable from a ban. A request for anything but a
testnet is refused: a faucet that can dispense value is not a faucet.

That last one is an invariant, not a policy. The faucet key must be
structurally unable to touch mainnet -- funded only on testnets, so the failure
mode of every bug in it is that testnet tokens move.

## What is not decided here

The allowance numbers, whether the drip is ETH and USDC or ETH only with USDC
minted, and whether a second draw tops up the same session address or issues a
new one. Those need the first draw to exist before they can be chosen on
evidence rather than guessed.

## Accepted 2026-09-01, with the on-chain half corrected

Owner direction: the self faucet should drop the CAPTCHA, be agent-first, and
ask for WebAuthn / passkey / smart-contract authentication instead. Accepted.

### Most of the gate already exists and is running

webauthn.adapters.edge verifies registration and assertion against WebCrypto,
and cloud-itonami/app-auth already uses it in production at
auth.itonami.cloud. The faucet does not implement ceremony crypto; it is a
relying party of what is live. org-w3-webauthn holds the ceremony data as EDN
with crypto injected, and org-nist-ecc has a P-256 verify that returns a
reason rather than a boolean, for hosts without WebCrypto.

### The smart-contract half is NOT available, and a session note said it was

The note said RIP-7212's P-256 precompile was verified working on Base. It is
not. Measured 2026-09-01 with the spec's exact layout -- address 0x100, 160
bytes of hash, r, s, x, y, 32 bytes of 1 on success and no output on failure
-- and with signatures verified valid locally before sending: Base, Base
Sepolia, Optimism, Arbitrum, Scroll and Ethereum all return nothing.

Separating absent from rejecting took work, because both are empty. The call
shape was validated first against precompiles that must exist: identity at
0x04 echoes its input and sha256 at 0x02 matches a locally computed digest.
With the shape proven, empty for a locally valid signature means absent.

A contract at 0xc2b78104907F722DABAc4C69f826a522B2754De4 exists on both Base
and Base Sepolia with 3537 bytes of code, and returned 0 for six independent
locally valid signatures, low-s normalised and not. Whether that is a
different contract or a different interface is UNVERIFIED; it is not evidence
that on-chain verification works.

scripts/p256-onchain-probe.cljs is the measurement, kept so the answer is
re-checkable rather than remembered. It exits 2 when it cannot validate its
own call shape, because an absence measured through a call that does not work
is not an absence.

### So the gate is a passkey, and the contract is the grant

Verification stays off-chain, in the relying party. What the passkey signs is
the spend grant of ADR-2609010100: scope, limit, expiry, revocable. The
facilitator enforces that grant on every settlement, so the agent's authority
is bounded by something a person authorised with a gesture, and bounded
without any contract needing to check a P-256 signature.

That is the trust boundary honestly stated: a holder of the passkey authorises
a grant, this relying party checks the assertion, and the facilitator refuses
anything outside the grant. Moving the check on-chain would remove the relying
party from the trusted set, and remains the goal -- blocked on P-256
verification that this workspace has now measured rather than assumed.")))